Q: Lifecycle Management: Safely Unsubscribing from HttpClient in Root-Provided Angular Signal Services A: <p>Use <code>toSignal</code> to convert the API to a signal, the cleanup is automatic and code is reduced.</p>
<pre><code>...
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class DataService {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);
  private sub: Subject&lt;void&gt; = new Subject&lt;void&gt;();
  // Reactive state
  private readonly #items = toSignal(this.sub.pipe(
    switchMap(() =&gt; this.http.get('/api/data'))
  ));
  readonly items = this.#items.asReadonly();

  fetchData() {
    this.sub.next();
  }
</code></pre>

Q: Extend signal form&#39;s FormField directive A: <p>In Angular we have two approach to &quot;enchance a directive&quot;.</p>
<p>1.-Extends your new directive from another one</p>
<pre><code>@Directive({
  selector: '[axFormField]',
})
export class AxFormFieldDirective&lt;T&gt; extends FormField&lt;T&gt; {..}

//And you use 
&lt;input [axFormField]=&quot;form.name&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>2.-Extends the &quot;properties&quot; of the tag.</p>
<pre><code>//see that you not use &quot;providers&quot;
@Directive({
  //the selector can be simply [axForField], but if you
  //use [formField][axFormField] you be sure it's apply only if have a formField
  selector: '[formField][axFormField]'
})
export class AxFormFieldDirective{
  formfield=inject(FormField)
}
//And you sue
&lt;input axFormField [formField]=&quot;form.name&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>Even you can use the same selector to makes all more transparent</p>
<pre><code>@Directive({
  selector: '[formField]'
})
export class AxFormFieldDirective{
  formfield=inject(FormField)
}
//And you sue
&lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.name&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>Since FormField is experimental (in Angular 21.0.0 is exporteAs <code>field</code>, in Angular 21.1.0 is exportedAs <code>formField</code>, in Angular 22.0.0 change again (really it's a good job, but it's crazy so many changes) the best bet is use the second approach.</p>
<p>BTW, as Mathie says, since Angular 21.0.0 you can use</p>
<pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App,{
  providers: [
    provideSignalFormsConfig({
      classes: NG_STATUS_CLASSES,
    }),
  ]});

</code></pre>
<p>And Angular add the class ng-valid,ng-invalud, ng-touched,...</p>
<p>or use customs class,e.g.</p>
<pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App,{
  providers: [
    provideSignalFormsConfig({
      classes: {
  'touched': ({ state }) =&gt; state.touched(),
  'untouched': ({ state }) =&gt; !state.touched(),
  'dirty': ({ state }) =&gt; state.dirty(),
  'pristine': ({ state }) =&gt; !state.dirty(),
  'valid': ({ state }) =&gt; state.valid(),
  'invalid': ({ state }) =&gt; state.invalid(),
  'pending': ({ state }) =&gt; state.pending(),
};
    }),
  ]});


</code></pre>

Q: One signal vs. multiple derived signals for complex api responses? A: <p>Personlly, I prefer one unique signal:</p>
<pre><code>export interface Complex
{
   data:StoreResponse|null,
   storeOptions:{label: string, value: int}[],
   deliveryZonesMap:Record&lt;string, DropdownOption[]&gt;
   paymentMethod:Record&lt;string, DropdownOption[]&gt;

}

private readonly data:signal&lt;Complex|null&gt;(null)

//you use data()?.data
//        data()?.storeOptions,
//        ....
</code></pre>

Q: Angular Signals: effect() error when used in afterNextRender A: <blockquote>
<p>effect() must be run in injection context, unless the injector option is manually specified.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In general you simply use the constructor of the component but not under afterNextRender</p>
<pre><code>constructor() {
  effect(() =&gt; {
     console.log(`counter value : ${this.counter()}`);
  })
}
</code></pre>
<p>Well, always can run using runInInjectionContext in the way</p>
<pre><code> injector = inject(Injector); //declare the injector

  constructor() {
    afterNextRender(() =&gt; {
      runInInjectionContext(this.injector, () =&gt; {
      effect(() =&gt; {
        console.log(`counter value : ${this.counter()}`);
      });
    })
    });
  }

</code></pre>
<p>but really I really forget this work-around</p>
<p>NOTE: you can use ngAfterViewInit instead of the afterNextRender function</p>
<pre><code>export class App implements AfterViewInit{

 injector = inject(Injector); //declare the injector

 constructor() {}

 ngAfterViewInit(){
   runInInjectionContext(this.injector, () =&gt; {
      effect(() =&gt; {
        console.log(`counter value : ${this.counter()}`);
      });
    });
  }

}
</code></pre>
<p>UPDATE: thanks the davood's comment. using <a href="https://angular.dev/api/core/AfterRenderOptions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">AfterRenderOptions</a> to inject the injector</p>
<pre><code>afterNextRender(() =&gt; {  
   effect(() =&gt; { 
     console.log(this.counter()); 
  }, 
  { injector: this.injector          }) 
})
</code></pre>

Q: Multiple instances of angular component react to changes from other instances A: <p><strong>use <code>Symbol()</code> per component instance.</strong></p>
<p>Each component creates its own unique ID automatically and no manual IDs needed.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// service
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class EditCoordinator {
  active = signal&lt;symbol | null&gt;(null);
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>// component
const id = Symbol();

isDisabled = computed(() =&gt;
  this.coordinator.active() !== null &amp;&amp;
  this.coordinator.active() !== id
);

edit() {
  this.coordinator.active.set(id);
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>Symbol()</code> is always unique and Component won’t react to its own updates.</p>

Q: Migration from Reactive Forms to Signal Forms bottom-up A: <p>There're not guide and it's not possible make it &quot;bite a bite&quot;. But, really I can not imagine a form with more than 50 variables</p>
<p>You should:</p>
<p>1.-Transform your custom form controls (When I say custom form control are NOT a group of inputs, else some of kid a counter, a calendar, a custom checkbox...)</p>
<p>2.-Get the whole form and create a <code>dataModel</code>and a <code>form</code>. See that both are signals</p>
<p>3.-In the form, that should be in the &quot;parent&quot; you define ALL. Yes, there're an unique &quot;Single source of truth&quot;.</p>
<p>4.-The validators are defined also in an unique place (where the form is defined), See that you define in the way:</p>
<pre><code>form = form(this.data, (schemaPath) =&gt; {
    required(schemaPath.id, { message: 'id is required' });
    required(schemaPath.login.password, { message: 'password is required' });
    ...
}

</code></pre>
<p>See that the validation, cross validation... are more &quot;robust&quot; and more &quot;simple&quot;</p>
<p>5.- If you have some like</p>
<pre><code>data = signal&lt;FormData&gt;({
    id: 'id',
    login: { email: 'email@email.com', password: 'password'},
  });
form=form(data);

</code></pre>
<p>And in .html</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
    &lt;label&gt;Id&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.id&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.login.email&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Password&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.login.password&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>To create an &quot;login.component&quot; you not use &quot;provider&quot; or pass as input any FormGroup (you have no a formGroup) else pass as input the FormTree</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
    &lt;label&gt;Id&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.id&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;login-component [group]=&quot;form.login&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>And your login-component like</p>
<pre><code>group=input&lt;any&gt;(null)  //&lt;--see that you get the input using the new &quot;input&quot; signal

&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input [formField]=&quot;group().email&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot;&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Password&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input [formField]=&quot;group().password&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>See that use group(), the <code>()</code>it's because &quot;group&quot; is an input -a signal-</p>
<p>6.- If you use the class ng-valid, ng-invalid, ng-touched... use</p>
<pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    provideSignalFormsConfig({
      classes: NG_STATUS_CLASSES,
    }),
  ],
});
</code></pre>
<p>7.- Take a breath and use the migration to better .css and .html, e.g. a simple</p>
<pre><code>input ~ .error
{
   color:red;
   font-size:.8rem;
   display:none
}
input.ng-invalid.ng-touched ~ .error
{
   display:block
}
</code></pre>
<p>In a form</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;formGroup&quot;&gt;
   &lt;label&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
   &lt;input [formField]=&quot;form.login.email&quot;&gt;
   &lt;div class=&quot;error&quot;&gt;
     {{form.login.email.errors()[0]?.message}}
   &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>makes the &quot;error&quot; appear without use <code>@if touched</code> or another, only .css</p>
<p>8.-Good luke!</p>
